# MAGIC %md-sandbox
# MAGIC ##### EXPAND FOR ADDITIONAL NOTES
# MAGIC
# MAGIC <details>
# MAGIC
# MAGIC A Genie Agent is made up of six context components. Only tables are required.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC #### Unity Catalog Tables (Required)
# MAGIC You select Unity Catalog tables and views to give Genie its default data context, but Genie is not strictly limited to only those objects. It can query other tables when users prompt for joins, edit SQL directly, or when outside tables are referenced in instructions or metadata. Access is governed by Unity Catalog permissions, so the real boundary is data entitlement, not just the list of tables attached to the space.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC
# MAGIC #### Table and Column Metadata (Strongly Recommended)
# MAGIC Descriptions and synonyms at table/column level, stored in Unity Catalog and/or the Genie **Knowledge Store**.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC - Critical for mapping natural language terms to the right columns and values
# MAGIC - Disambiguates similar fields (e.g., customer `country` vs. franchise `country`)
# MAGIC - For example, a column named `size` with values `S`, `M`, `L`, `XL` is ambiguous without a description
# MAGIC - Adding "Franchise size category. Values: S (Small), M (Medium), L (Large), XL (Extra Large)" tells Genie exactly how to interpret it
# MAGIC - The Knowledge Store also provides **prompt matching** capabilities (format assistance and entity matching) that help Genie map user language to actual data values
# MAGIC
# MAGIC > **Note:** The Knowledge Store is covered in detail in the next lecture. For now, think of it as the centralized place within a Genie Agent where you configure all curation settings.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC
# MAGIC #### SQL Logic: Joins, Expressions, Queries (Highly Recommended)
# MAGIC This is the primary way Genie learns your business logic. Docs explicitly say to **prioritize SQL expressions and example SQL over text instructions** for business semantics.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC - **Joins / relationships** — Prefer PK/FK constraints in UC or pre-joined views/metric views rather than describing joins in text
# MAGIC - **SQL expressions / functions** — Define business terms and metric calculations in SQL (e.g., revenue = `SUM(totalPrice)`), or register them as UC functions
# MAGIC - **Example SQL queries** — Pair natural language questions with the SQL that answers them. Genie uses these as trusted patterns when generating queries
# MAGIC
# MAGIC #### Instructions (Text) - Behavior & Edge Cases (Recommended, but "last resort")
# MAGIC Use text instructions for things that **cannot be expressed cleanly in SQL or metadata**:
# MAGIC
# MAGIC - When to ask clarifying questions
# MAGIC - How to format answers (rounding, language, row limits)
# MAGIC - What to do when users omit key filters (time range, region, etc.)
# MAGIC
# MAGIC **Avoid** using Instructions to define metrics, joins, or filters if you can express them in SQL instead.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC
# MAGIC #### Common Questions (Recommended)
# MAGIC Example business questions that teach Genie common question patterns. These can serve two purposes:
# MAGIC
# MAGIC 1. **Guide users** — Common questions appear in the UI as suggestions for users, showing users what kinds of questions they can ask  
# MAGIC 2. **Guide Genie** — You can take common questions and add them as **SQL Queries** to act as examples that help Genie generate better SQL
# MAGIC
# MAGIC **Best practice:** Attach SQL answers when possible so they become example queries, not just free-floating questions.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC
# MAGIC #### Benchmarks (Strongly Recommended)
# MAGIC Structured test suites that let you measure and track Genie's accuracy over time.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC - Save key questions with optional **ground truth SQL** that defines the expected correct answer
# MAGIC - Run benchmarks after every change (descriptions, instructions, example SQL) to confirm improvements and catch regressions
# MAGIC - Each benchmark question runs as a **new, independent conversation** — no thread context is carried over, simulating a fresh user interaction
# MAGIC - Essential for the **test, benchmark, and iterate** cycle that turns a good Genie Agent into a great one
# MAGIC
# MAGIC > **Note:** Benchmarks do **not** change how Genie answers questions. They are purely for evaluation and quality tracking.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC </details>

